  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+3}+\\frac{C}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+11x+7=A(x+3)(x+4)+B(x-1)(x+4)+C(x-1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+11(1)+7 & =A(1+3)(1+4)+B(1-1)(1+4)+C(1-1)(1+3) \\\\ 20 & =20A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-3)^{2}+11(-3)+7 & =A(-3+3)(-3+4)+B(-3-1)(-3+4)+C(-3-1)(-3+3) \\\\ -8 & =-4B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)^{2}+11(-4)+7 & =A(-4+3)(-4+4)+B(-4-1)(-4+4)+C(-4-1)(-4+3) \\\\ -5 & =5C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+11x+7}{(x-1)(x+3)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{2}{x+3}-\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$",
